  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-215740",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $3.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{1}{3}y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+y=18 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\cancel{y} & =18 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =4 \\\\ 11x & =22 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $6x+y=18$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+y & =18 \\\\ 6\\cdot 2+y & =18 \\\\ 12+y & =18 \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
